      },
      "answer": "Yes, this change introduces a critical SQL injection vulnerability.\n\nThe original code used proper parameterized queries with `?` placeholders and separate parameter binding, which safely handles user input by treating it as data rather than executable SQL code.\n\nThe new code uses string formatting (`%s`) to directly insert the email parameter into the SQL query. This allows malicious users to inject arbitrary SQL commands through the email parameter, potentially leading to data theft, data corruption, or even complete database compromise.\n\nFor example, if an attacker passes `\"; DROP TABLE customers; --` as the email parameter, it would execute the destructive SQL command.\n\nThe fix is to revert to using parameterized queries with proper placeholder binding instead of string formatting.",
