      "answer": "Based on my analysis, this change introduces a **security vulnerability**.\n\nThe original code used `subprocess.run([\"tar\", \"czf\", \"backup.tgz\", name])` which safely passes arguments as separate list elements, preventing command injection attacks.\n\nThe changed code uses `subprocess.run(\"tar czf backup.tgz \" + name, shell=True)` which:\n1. Concatenates the command and argument into a single string\n2. Uses `shell=True`, making it vulnerable to shell injection\n3. Allows malicious input in the `name` parameter to be interpreted as shell commands\n\nIf an attacker can control the `name` parameter, they could inject shell commands that would be executed with the privileges of the process running this code. For example, if `name` contained `\"; rm -rf /\"`, it would execute that command along with the tar command.\n\nThis is a classic command injection vulnerability that should be fixed by reverting to the original safe approach or properly escaping the input before concatenation.",
