      "answer": "# Security Vulnerability Review\n\nYes, this change **introduces a critical SQL injection vulnerability**.\n\n## The Problem\n\nThe diff shows a shift from safe parameterized queries to unsafe string interpolation:\n\n**Before (safe):**\n```python\nq = \"SELECT id, name FROM customers WHERE email = ?\"\nreturn conn.execute(q, (email,)).fetchone()\n```\n\n**After (vulnerable):**\n```python\nq = \"SELECT id, name FROM customers WHERE email = '%s'\" % email\nreturn conn.execute(q).fetchone()\n```\n\n## Why This Is Dangerous\n\n- **Old code**: Uses `?` placeholder with parameterized query. The database driver safely escapes the `email` value, treating it as data only \u2014 never executable SQL.\n\n- **New code**: Uses `%s` string formatting to directly interpolate `email` into the SQL string. An attacker can inject arbitrary SQL by supplying crafted input like:\n  - `' OR '1'='1`\n  - `' UNION SELECT id, password, ... FROM users --`\n  - `'; DROP TABLE customers --`\n\n## Impact\n\nAn attacker could bypass authentication, extract sensitive data (passwords, other customers' records), or potentially modify/delete data depending on the database backend and permissions.\n\nThis is a well-documented security anti-pattern. The fix is to revert to parameterized queries with `?` placeholders.",
